-- title:
--   A section missing a closed subscheme induces the unit ideal
-- statement:
--   Let $O$ be a commutative local ring, $X$ a scheme, and $\sigma : \operatorname{Spec} O \to X$ a closed immersion. Let $J$ be quasi-coherent ideal sheaf data on $X$, and assume that the image under $\sigma$ of the closed point of $O$ does not lie in the support of $J$. Let $k$ be a field and let $y$ be any morphism from the closed subscheme $V(J)$ cut out by $J$ to $\operatorname{Spec} k$. Write $I$ for the inverse image $\sigma.\mathrm{ker}$ of the kernel ideal sheaf of $\sigma$ along the closed immersion $J.\mathrm{subschemeι} : V(J) \to X$. The conclusion is fourfold: $I$ is the unit ideal sheaf $\top$ on $V(J)$; $I$ is invertible in the sense that every point of $V(J)$ lies in some basic open $D(f)$, $f \in \Gamma(V(J), U)$ for an affine open $U$, such that on the affine basic open attached to $f$ the ideal of $I$ is generated by a single non-zero-divisor; the composite of the closed immersion of $V(I) \subseteq V(J)$ with $y$ is a finite morphism; and for every point $q$ of $\operatorname{Spec} k$ the rank of that composite at $q$ is $0$.
--
--   This is the degenerate (degree-zero) case of the computation of degrees of ideal sheaves restricted to a closed subscheme: if a section of a scheme over a local ring meets a closed subscheme nowhere, its kernel ideal restricts to the unit ideal there, so the associated subscheme is empty and contributes nothing. It supplies the vanishing term in an Euler-characteristic identity for pullbacks of ideal sheaves used on a resolved model of a modular curve.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_Scheme_Hom_comap_ker_eq_top_and_finrank_eq_zero_of_closedPoint_notMem_support.lean

